WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.580
But if you want to get into the nitty-gritty -- the down-and-dirty -- of how you actually

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:05.560
move, well, there are rules.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.480
Really, just two main rules, and they have to do with proteins.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.620
And they’re both true for a lot of the proteins we talk about, whether they’re enzymes or

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.180
ion channels or receptors or muscle proteins.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.600
And these rules are:

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.180
One. Proteins like to change shape when stuff binds to them. And two. Changing shapes can

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.280
allow proteins to bind -- or unbind -- with other stuff.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.740
So keep those rules in mind, while we see how a muscle fiber contracts and relaxes.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.420
Now, remember those tiny myofibrils that bundle up to form your muscle fibers?

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.700
They’re divided lengthwise into segments called sarcomeres, which contain two even

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:43.180
tinier strands of protein -- two different kinds of myofilaments called actin and myosin.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.380
And it’s their angsty story of star-crossed love that fuels every movement your body could possibly dream up.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.440
A sarcomere contains both thin filaments, made up mostly of two light and twisty actin

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.280
strands, and thick filaments, composed of thicker, lumpy-looking myosin strands.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.720
Each sarcomere is separated by what’s known as a Z line at either end, which is just a

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.560
border formed by alternating thin filaments in a kind of zig zag pattern.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.280
A muscle contracting is all about sarcomeres contracting, bringing those Z-lines closer together.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.900
All right, so now comes the romance.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:19.560
When your muscle cells are at rest, your actin & myosin strands don’t touch, but they really, really want to.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.200
Specifically, that club-headed myosin wants to get all up-close-and-personal with the actin.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:29.500
When this happens -- and it will, eventually -- it’s called the sliding filament model of muscle contraction.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.760
But in the meantime, like in any good love story, the pair have some obstacles to overcome.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.060
Namely, actin is blocked by a couple of protein bodyguards -- called tropomyosin and troponin

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.740
-- which keep getting in the way.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:43.780
Luckily, these guards can be bought off with a little ATP and some calcium.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.520
I prefer cash and nachos, but whatever.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:51.560
Remember, ATP is kind of like molecular currency. It contains chemical energy, and your muscles

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.760
are all about converting chemical energy to motion, so they’re always hungry for more ATP.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.860
Your muscle cells have lots of nuclei, but some of them also have a lot of mitochondria,

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.400
whose sole purpose in life is to crank out ATP.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.720
And muscle cells also have their own version of an endoplasmic reticulum -- the cell’s

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.920
transport and storage system -- but in this case it’s specialized, so it gets a special

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.220
name: the sarcoplasmic reticulum.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.880
Its walls are loaded with calcium pumps -- which use ATP to save up a bunch of calcium ions.
